Yes. I would treat this as a quantitative annotation / decision-metric problem rather than simply trying to put more numbers onto the four plots. The meeting surfaced several candidate calculations, but they are not all equally useful.
1. Quantitative comparisons that came up
The most directly requested calculation was a storage margin relative to Company Plan (CP26). You explicitly asked whether it would help to quantify the delta between, for example, the P50 storage outcome and CP26 around 2030 for each case. Shelby's response was essentially: yes, if it is easy to calculate; worst case it can go in the notes rather than cluttering the graphic. The discussion then converged on expressing it as excess vs. shortfall relative to CP26. Voice 261007_105900_original.txt
I would therefore put that at the top of the quantitative-analysis list.
A second family of calculations is confidence / percentile threshold for meeting Company Plan. This actually gets closer to Shelby's repeated wording about Daisy allowing the portfolio to "confidently meet Company Plan". Voice 261007_105900_original.txt Instead of merely reporting P50 capacity, you can ask, for each scenario and year:
At P10, does storage meet CP26?
At P50?
At P90?
What is the lowest storage percentile/outcome that still meets CP26?
Conversely, approximately what fraction of the modeled storage-outcome range clears CP26?
That could turn "confidently" from qualitative language into something quantitatively defensible.
Third is the analogous comparison against Strategic Goals. Shelby specifically noticed that in the Louisiana-success case the more interesting Daisy story may be that it moves the portfolio toward—or potentially allows it to meet—Strategic Goals with high confidence. Another participant cautioned that Company Plan is the main driver and Strategic Goals represent additional capacity/upside. Voice 261007_105900_original.txt
So calculate the same margin against both reference lines:
Storage margin = modeled storage capacity − target demand
for both:
CP26
Strategic Goals
at P10 / P50 / P90.
Fourth is the incremental capacity attributable to Daisy. Because the panels are paired—with/without Daisy under the same Louisiana assumption—you can calculate:
Daisy uplift = storage outcome with Daisy − storage outcome without Daisy
for each percentile and year.
That gives you questions such as:
How much capacity does Daisy add at P50 in 2030, 2031, and 2032?
Is the uplift broadly constant across P10–P90, or does Daisy disproportionately improve the downside?
How much does Daisy reduce the CP26 shortfall in the LA-failure case?
How much additional headroom does it provide in the LA-success case?
This number was not requested as explicitly as the P50-vs-CP26 delta, but it is a natural quantitative expression of the entire slide title, "Daisy's Impact on Risked Storage Outlook."
Fifth is when Daisy first becomes necessary. The meeting repeatedly shifted from "how much" toward "when." One participant cited an existing statement that CP26 apparently does not require Daisy until a particular future year, although that section of the transcript is garbled enough that I would not trust the transcribed year without returning to the source data. Voice 261007_105900_original.txt The group also emphasized that acceleration before demand requires it could be economically undesirable. Voice 261007_105900_original.txt
For each percentile, you could compute:
First year without Daisy in which CP26 exceeds available storage
and then compare it with the Daisy startup date.
That produces a very decision-oriented metric:
Daisy first becomes capacity-constrained / required in YEAR under Pxx.
Sixth is the timing/cost tradeoff, but I would treat this as a separate analysis because it requires economic/infrastructure data rather than only these simulation outputs. The discussion suggests:
waiting until approximately the existing Sunflower profile declines may avoid a booster;
accelerating Daisy may trigger additional surface/infrastructure CapEx;
one participant recalled the acceleration changing storage cost by roughly $3 per unit, but the transcript is too garbled to establish the exact unit/value confidently;
several other dollar/volume numbers in this portion are also transcription-corrupted and should not be copied from the transcript into an analysis. Voice 261007_105900_original.txt Voice 261007_105900_original.txt
